(2623, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions </b>\r\nIn each of the following questions, there are three words which are related in \r\nsome way. The relationship in each case is indicated by one of the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given below. The alternative which best states the relationship is the answer. <br><br>\r\nSinger, Writer, Actor\r\n', 'P includes part of Q and part of R but Q and R are independent of each other', 'P includes Q and part of R but Q is independent of R', 'P, Q and R include parts of one another', 'P includes both Q and R.', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2624, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions </b>\r\nIn each of the following questions, there are three words which are related in \r\nsome way. The relationship in each case is indicated by one of the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given below. The alternative which best states the relationship is the answer. <br><br>\r\nSoldier, Army, Engineer\r\n', 'P includes part of Q and part of R but Q and R are independent of each other', 'P includes Q and part of R but Q is independent of R', 'P, Q and R include parts of one another', 'P includes both Q and R.', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1);
INSERT INTO `qb_options` (`id`, `cat_id`, `difficulty_level`, `question`, `answer1`, `answer2`, `answer3`, `answer4`, `answer5`, `option1`, `option2`, `option3`, `option4`, `option5`, `status`) VALUES
(2625, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions \r\nIn each of the following questions, there are three words which are related in \r\nsome way. The relationship in each case is indicated by one of the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given below. The alternative which best states the relationship is the answer. <br><br>\r\nTiger, Elephant, Quadruped\r\n', 'P includes part of Q and part of R but Q and R are independent of each other', 'P includes Q and part of R but Q is independent of R', 'P, Q and R include parts of one another', 'P includes both Q and R.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2626, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions </b>\r\nIn each of the following questions, there are three words which are related in some way. The relationship in each case is indicated by one of the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given below. The alternative which best states the relationship is the answer. <br><br>\r\nResearcher, Historian, Scholar\r\n', 'P includes part of Q and part of R but Q and R are independent of each other', 'P includes Q and part of R but Q is independent of R', 'P, Q and R include parts of one another', 'P includes both Q and R.', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2627, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> \r\n<br>Answer the questions on your own. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThe total no. of numbers that are divisible by 2 or 3 between \r\n100 and 200(both inclusive) are:\r\n', '67', '57', '77', '47', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2628, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> 26 to 30: <br>Answer the questions on your own. <br><br>\r\nFrom a pack of cards Jack, Queen, King and Ace are removed. Then the algebraic sum of rest of the cards is:\r\n', '216', '214', '210', '220', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2630, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>\r\n: <br>Answer the questions on your own. <br><br>\r\nThe average temperature of days from Monday to Wednesday is 37 degree Celsius and that of from Tuesday to Thursday is 34 degrees. The temperature of Thursday is 4/5th of Monday. Then the temperature of Thursday is? (In degrees)\r\n', '32 Degree Celsius', '30 Degree Celsius', '34 Degree Celsius', '36 Degree Celsius', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2631, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> \r\n<br>Answer the questions on your own. <br><br>\r\n''Bâ€™ is 50% faster than â€˜Aâ€™. If â€˜Aâ€™ starts at 9 A.M. and â€˜Bâ€™ starts at 10 A.M. â€˜Aâ€™ travels at a speed of 50 km/hr. If â€˜Aâ€™ and â€˜Bâ€™ are 300 km apart, at what time when they meet when they travel in opposite direction is? \r\n', '11 am ', '12 pm ', '9 pm 	', '8am', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2632, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> \r\n<br>Answer the questions on your own. <br><br>\r\nA cube of 12 mm is painted on all its sides. If it is made up of small cubes of size 3mm and the big cube is split into those small cubes, the number of cubes that remain unpainted is?\r\n', '9', '10', '8', '12', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2633, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>\r\nEach question given below has a problem and two statements numbered I and II giving certain information. You have to decide if the information given in the statements is sufficient for answering the problem. Indicate your answer as: <br><br>\r\nHow many New Yearâ€™s greeting cards were sold this year in your shop? <br>\r\n(I) Last year 2935 cards were sold. <br>\r\n(II) The number of cards sold this year was 1.2 times that of last year.\r\n', 'if the data in statement I alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in statement II alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in either in  I  or II alone are sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data even in both the statements together are not sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data in both the statements together are needed. ', 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1),
(2634, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> <br>\r\nEach question given below has a problem and two statements numbered I and II giving certain information. You have to decide if the information given in the statements is sufficient for answering the problem. Indicate your answer as: <br><br>\r\nHemant ranks tenth in a class. How many students are there in the class? <br>\r\n(I) His friend got 58th rank which is the last. <br>\r\n(II) Hemantâ€™s rank from the last 49th.\r\n', 'if the data in statement I alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in statement II alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in either in  I  or II alone are sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data even in both the statements together are not sufficient to answer the question;', ' if the data in both the statements together are needed. ', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2635, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> <br>\r\nEach question given below has a problem and two statements numbered I and II giving certain information. You have to decide if the information given in the statements is sufficient for answering the problem. Indicate your answer as: <br><br>\r\nIn a code, â€˜lee pee tinâ€™ means â€˜Always keep smilingâ€™. What is the code for â€˜smilingâ€™? <br>\r\n(I) â€˜tin lut leeâ€™ means â€˜Always keep leftâ€™<br>\r\n(II) â€˜dee peeâ€™ means â€˜rose smilingâ€™\r\n', 'if the data in statement I alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in statement II alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in either in  I  or II alone are sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data even in both the statements together are not sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data in both the statements together are needed. ', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2636, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> <br>\r\nEach question given below has a problem and two statements numbered I and II giving certain information. You have to decide if the information given in the statements is sufficient for answering the problem. Indicate your answer as: <br><br>\r\nAt what time did sonali leave her home for office? <br>\r\n(I) Sonali received a phone call at her home<br>\r\n(II) Sonaliâ€™s car reached office at 10.15 AM, 45 minutes after she left her home\r\n', 'if the data in statement I alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in statement II alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in either in  I  or II alone are sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data even in both the statements together are not sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data in both the statements together are needed. ', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2637, 241, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b> <br>\r\nEach question given below has a problem and two statements numbered I and II giving certain information. You have to decide if the information given in the statements is sufficient for answering the problem. Indicate your answer as: <br><br>\r\nA, B, C, D and E are sitting in a row. B is between A and E. Who among them is in the middle? <br>\r\n(I) A is left of B and right of D<br>\r\n(II) C is at the right end\r\n', 'if the data in statement I alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in statement II alone are sufficient to answer the question; ', 'if the data in either in  I  or II alone are sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data even in both the statements together are not sufficient to answer the question;', 'if the data in both the statements together are needed. ', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2638, 241, 2, 'A solid cube of each side 8 cm has been painted red, blue and black on pairs of opposite faces. It is then cut into cubical blocks of each side 2 cm. How many cubes have only one face painted?', '8', '16', '24', '28', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2639, 241, 2, 'A milkman mixes 20 litres of water with 80 litres of milk. After selling one-fourth of this mixture, he adds water to replenish the quantity that he has sold. What is the current proportion of water to milk?', '2:3', '1:2', '1:3', '3:4', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2640, 241, 2, 'If a man cycles at 10 km/hr, then he arrives at a certain place at 1 p.m. If he cycles at 15 km/hr, he will arrive at the same place at 11 a.m. At what speed must he cycle to get there at noon?', '11 km/hr', '12 km/hr', '13 km/hr        ', '14 km/hr', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2641, 241, 2, 'Two boats, traveling at 5 and 10 kms per hour, head directly\r\ntowards each other. They begin at a distance of 20 kms from\r\neach other. How far apart are they (in kms) one minute before\r\nthey collide?\r\n', ' 1/12	', '1/6      ', '1/4', '1/3', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2642, 241, 2, '3 small pumps and a large pump are filling a tank. Each of the three small pumps works at 2/3rd the rate of the large pump. If all 4 pumps work at the same time, they should fill the tank in what fraction of the time that it would have taken the large pump alone?', '4/7', '1/3', '2/3', '3/4', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2643, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>:<br> In each question below, two statements are given followed by two conclusions numbered I and II. You have to take the given two statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read the conclusion and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the two given statements, disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n<b> Statements: </b> Some papers are files.\r\nSome files are pens.<br>\r\n<b>Conclusions: </b> (I) some files are not pens.\r\n(II) Some pens are papers.\r\n', 'if only conclusion I follows;', 'if only conclusion II follows; ', 'if either I or II follows; ', 'if neither I nor II follows ', 'if both I and II follow.', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2644, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>:<br> In each question below, two statements are given followed by two conclusions numbered I and II. You have to take the given two statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read the conclusion and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the two given statements, disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>Statements: </b> All locks are keys.\r\nNo key is a spoon.<br>\r\n<b>Conclusions: </b> (I) No lock is a spoon<br>\r\n(II) No spoon is a lock\r\n', 'if only conclusion I follows;', 'if only conclusion II follows; ', 'if either I or II follows; ', 'if neither I nor II follows ', 'if both I and II follow.', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2645, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>:<br> In each question below, two statements are given followed by two conclusions numbered I and II. You have to take the given two statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read the conclusion and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the two given statements, disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n<b>Statements: </b> Some bottles are pencils\r\nSome pencils are glasses<br>\r\n<b>Conclusions: </b> (I) No glass is bottle<br>\r\n(II) Some bottles are glasses\r\n', 'if only conclusion I follows;', 'if only conclusion II follows; ', 'if either I or II follows; ', 'if both I and II follow.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2646, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>:<br> In each question below, two statements are given followed by two conclusions numbered I and II. You have to take the given two statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read the conclusion and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the two given statements, disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n<b>Statements: </b> Sohan is a good sportsman.\r\nSportsmen are healthy. <br>\r\n<b>Conclusions: </b> (I) All healthy persons are sportsmen. <br>\r\n(II) Sohan is healthy.\r\n', 'if only conclusion I follows;', 'if only conclusion II follows; ', 'if either I or II follows; ', 'if neither I nor II follows ', 'if both I and II follow.', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2647, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>: <br>In each questions below are given two statements followed by two conclusions numbered I and II. You have to take the given two statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. read the conclusion and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the two given statements, disregarding commonly known facts. <br>\r\n<b> Statements: </b> All green are blue.\r\nAll blue are white. <br>\r\n<b>Conclusions: </b> (I) Some blue are green. <br>\r\n(II) Some white are green. <br>\r\n(III) Some green are not white. <br>\r\n(IV) All white are blue.\r\n', 'Only I and II follow	', 'Only I and III follow.', 'Only I and IV follow', 'Only II and IV follow', 'All follow.', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2648, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions</b>: <br>In each questions below are given two statements followed by two conclusions numbered I and II. You have to take the given two statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. read the conclusion and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the two given statements, disregarding commonly known facts. <br>\r\n<b>Statements: </b> No parrot is crow.\r\nAll crows are bats. <br>\r\n<b>Conclusions: </b> (I) Some bats are parrots<br>\r\n(II) All bats are parrots 	<br>	(III) Some bats are crows<br>\r\n(IV) Some bats are not crows\r\n', 'None follows	', 'Only I and II follow.', 'Only I, II and III follow	', 'Only II, III and IV follow', 'Only III and IV follow', 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1),
(2649, 242, 2, '<b>Directions for Questions from</b>: In each of the following questions one word is different from the rest. Find out the word which does not belong to the group<br>', 'BE', 'HK	', 'NQ	', 'MN', 'WZ', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
