(4188, 306, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Given are two statements followed by four conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the two given statements to be true even if they seem to be variance with commonly known facts. Read the conclusions and then decide which of them logically follows from the statements. <br><br>\r\n<b>Statement I: </b> Some parrots are crows. <br>\r\n     <b>Statement II: </b> Some crows are sparrows. <br>\r\n    <b> Conclusions: </b>	<br>\r\n	          I.  Some parrots are sparrows. <br>\r\n	          II. No parrot is a sparrow. <br>\r\n	          III. All sparrows are parrots.\r\n', 'I or II', 'I or III', 'II or III', 'None', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(4189, 306, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Given are two statements followed by four conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the two given statements to be true even if they seem to be variance with commonly known facts. Read the conclusions and then decide which of them logically follows from the statements. <br><br>\r\n<b>Statement I: </b> All pens are pencils. <br>\r\n    <b>Statement II: </b>All pencils are scales. <br>\r\n    <b>Conclusions: </b>	<br>\r\n	         I.  All pens are scales. <br>\r\n	         II. All scales are pencils. <br>\r\n	         III. All scales are pens.\r\n', 'I', 'II', 'I and II', 'I,II and III', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(4186, 306, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Given are two statements followed by four conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the two given statements to be true even if they seem to be variance with commonly known facts. Read the conclusions and then decide which of them logically follows from the statements. <br><br>\r\n<b>Statement I: </b>Both Vinod and Vinodini are shorter than \r\n     Shama. <br>\r\n     <b>Statement II: </b> Shama is not the tallest of the four friends \r\n     Vinod, Vinodini, Shama and Tara. <br>\r\n     <b> Conclusions: </b><br>\r\n	         I. Tara is shorter than Vinod. <br>\r\n	         II. Either Vinod or Vinodini is taller than Tara. <br>\r\n	         III. Shama is the second tallest among the \r\n                           four friends.\r\n', 'I', 'II', 'II', 'I and II', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(4187, 306, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Given are two statements followed by four conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the two given statements to be true even if they seem to be variance with commonly known facts. Read the conclusions and then decide which of them logically follows from the statements. <br><br>\r\n<b>Statement I: </b> No woman is a sailor. <br>\r\n    <b> Statement II: </b> Lathika is a woman.	<br>	\r\n     <b>Conclusions: </b>	<br>\r\n	          I.  Lathika is not a sailor. <br>\r\n	          II. Lathika is a sailor. <br>\r\n 	          III. Only men are sailors.\r\n', 'I', 'II', 'I and II', 'I and III', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
