(4139, 302, 2, '<b>Directions to Solve</b><br>\r\nHarold a professional man who had worked in an office for many years had a fearful dream. In it, he found himself in a land where small slug-like animals with slimy tentacles lived on people''s bodies. The people tolerated the loathsome creatures because after many years they grew into elephants which then became the nation''s system of transport, carrying everyone wherever he wanted to go. Harold suddenly realised that he himself was covered with these things, and he woke up screaming. In a vivid sequence of pictures this dream dramatised for Harold what he had never been able to put in to words; he saw himself as letting society feed on his body in his early years so that it would carry him when he retired. He later threw off the "security bug" and took up freelance work.<br>\r\n\r\nWhich one of the following phrases best helps to bring out the precise meaning of ''loathsome creatures''?', 'Security bug and slimy tentacles', 'Fearful dream and slug-like animals', 'Slimy tentacles and slug-like animals', 'slug-like animals and security bug', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(4137, 302, 2, '<b>Directions to Solve</b><br>\r\nI felt the wall of the tunnel shiver. The master alarm squealed through my earphones. Almost simultaneously, Jack yelled down to me that there was a warning light on. Fleeting but spectacular sights snapped into ans out of view, the snow, the shower of debris, the moon, looming close and big, the dazzling sunshine for once unfiltered by layers of air. The last twelve hours before re-entry were particular bone-chilling. During this period, I had to go up in to command module. Even after the fiery re-entry splashing down in 81o water in south pacific, we could still see our frosty breath inside the command module.\r\n\r\n<br>The statement that the dazzling sunshine was "for once unfiltered by layers of air" means', 'that the sun was very hot', 'that there was no strong wind', 'that the air was unpolluted', 'none of above', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(4138, 302, 2, '<b>Directions to Solve</b><br>\r\nHarold a professional man who had worked in an office for many years had a fearful dream. In it, he found himself in a land where small slug-like animals with slimy tentacles lived on people''s bodies. The people tolerated the loathsome creatures because after many years they grew into elephants which then became the nation''s system of transport, carrying everyone wherever he wanted to go. Harold suddenly realised that he himself was covered with these things, and he woke up screaming. In a vivid sequence of pictures this dream dramatised for Harold what he had never been able to put in to words; he saw himself as letting society feed on his body in his early years so that it would carry him when he retired. He later threw off the "security bug" and took up freelance work.\r\n<br><br>\r\nIn his dream Harold found the loathsome creatures', 'in his village', 'in his own house', 'in a different land', 'in his office', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(2997, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> In each question, a part\r\nof sentence is printed in italics. Below each sentence, some\r\nphrases are given which can substitute the italicized part of\r\nthe sentence. If the sentence is correct as it is, the answer\r\nis ''No correction required''<br><br>\r\nIt is time for the six year old is learning how to read and\r\nwrite \r\n', 'has learned ', 'to learn', 'was learning ', 'No correction required ', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(2998, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> In each question, a part\r\nof sentence is printed in italics. Below each sentence, some\r\nphrases are given which can substitute the italicized part of\r\nthe sentence. If the sentence is correct as it is, the answer\r\nis ''No correction required''<br><br>\r\nI am sorry, but I don''t believe in what you say. \r\n', 'I will not believe ', 'I am not believing ', 'I will not be believing ', 'No correction required ', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(2999, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> In each question, a part\r\nof sentence is printed in italics. Below each sentence, some\r\nphrases are given which can substitute the italicized part of\r\nthe sentence. If the sentence is correct as it is, the answer\r\nis ''No correction required''<br><br>\r\nShe could not help laugh. \r\n', 'laughing', 'laughed ', 'laugh 	', 'No correction required ', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3000, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> In each question, a part\r\nof sentence is printed in italics. Below each sentence, some\r\nphrases are given which can substitute the italicized part of\r\nthe sentence. If the sentence is correct as it is, the answer\r\nis ''No correction required''<br><br>\r\nHe never tells the truth \r\n', 'Says 	', 'utters ', 'speaks', 'No correction required ', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3001, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> In each question, a part\r\nof sentence is printed in italics. Below each sentence, some\r\nphrases are given which can substitute the italicized part of\r\nthe sentence. If the sentence is correct as it is, the answer\r\nis ''No correction required''<br><br>\r\nSince 1986, there is no earthquakes here \r\n', 'have been ', 'are ', 'from 	', 'No correction required', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3002, 260, 2, '<b>Direction for the question:</b><br> Read and\r\nunderstand the paragraph carefully and choose one of the\r\nstatements given below that supports the question. <br><br>\r\nThe press should not be afraid of upholding and\r\nsupporting a just and righteous cause. It should not be\r\nafraid of criticising the government in a healthy manner. The\r\npress has to be eternally vigilant to protect the rights of the\r\nworkers, backward and suppressed sections of the society.\r\nIt should also give a balanced view of the things so that\r\npeople can be helped in the formation of a healthy public\r\nopinion. <br>\r\n The passage best supports the statement that\r\n', 'the freedom of press is essential for the proper\r\nfunctioning of democracy.	\r\n', 'the press is the only means to project to the masses the\r\npolicies of the government.\r\n', 'press has a great role to play in a democracy.	', 'the press can be used by the governments as an\r\neffective media for the upliftment of the backward sections\r\nof society.\r\n', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3003, 260, 2, '<b>Direction for the question:</b><br> Read and\r\nunderstand the paragraph carefully and choose one of the\r\nstatements given below that supports the question. <br><br>\r\nThe only true education comes through the stimulation\r\nof the child''s power by the demands of the social situations\r\nin which he finds himself. Through these demands he is\r\nstimulated to act as a member of a unity, to emerge from\r\nhis original narrowness of action and feeling, and to\r\nconceive himself from the standpoint of the welfare of the\r\ngroup to which he belongs.<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe passage best supports the statement that real education.\r\n', 'will take place if the children imbibe action and feeling.', 'will take place if the children are physically strong', 'is not provided in our schools today', 'comes through the interaction with social situations', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3004, 260, 2, '<b>Direction for the questions:</b><br> Find the correct order<br><br>\r\n\r\nA. But this does not mean that death was the Egyptiansâ€™ only preoccupation. <br>       \r\nB. Even papyri come mainly from pyramid temples.  <br>     \r\nC. Most of our traditional sources of information about the Old Kingdom are monuments of the rich like pyramids and tombs.       <br>\r\nD. Houses in which ordinary Egyptians lived have not been preserved, and when most people died they were buried in simple graves.       <br> \r\nE. We know infinitely more about the wealthy people of Egypt than we do about the ordinary people, as most monuments were made for the rich.        \r\n', 'CDBEA', 'ECDAB', 'EDCBA ', 'DECAB', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3005, 260, 2, '<b>Direction for the questions:</b><br> Find the correct order<br><br>\r\nA. Too much of the Labour movement, it symbolises the brutality of the upper classes.        \r\nB. And to everybody watching, the current mess over foxhunting symbolises the governmentâ€™s weakness.       \r\nC. To foxhuntingâ€™s supporters, Labourâ€™s 1991 manifesto commitment to ban it symbolises the partyâ€™s metropolitan roots and hostility to the countryside.        \r\nD. Small issues sometimes have large symbolic power.       \r\nE. To those who enjoy thundering across the countryside in red coats after foxes, foxhunting symbolises the ancient roots of rural lives.        \r\n', 'DEACB', 'ECDBA', 'CEADB  ', 'DBAEC', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3006, 260, 2, ' <b>Direction for the questions:</b><br> Find the correct order<br><br>\r\n\r\nA. In the case of King Merolchazzarâ€™s courtship of the Princess of the Outer Isles, there occurs a regrettable hitch.       <br>\r\nB. She acknowledges the gifts, but no word of a meeting date follows.    <br>    \r\nC. The monarch, hearing good reports of a neighbouring princess, dispatches messengers with gifts to her court, beseeching an interview.      <br>        \r\nD. The princess names a date, and a formal meeting takes place; after that everything buzzes along pretty smoothly.       <br>\r\nE. Royal love affairs in olden days were conducted on the correspondence method.        ', 'ACBDE', 'ABCDE ', 'ECDAB ', 'ECBAD', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3007, 260, 2, ' <b>Direction for the questions:</b><br> Find the correct order<br><br>\r\nA.  Who can trace to its first beginnings the love of Damon for Pythias, of David for Jonathan, of Swan for Edgar?   <br>     \r\nB. Similarly with men.    <br>   \r\nC. There is about great friendships between man and man a certain inevitability that can only be compared with the age- old association of ham and eggs.      <br>  \r\n\r\n\r\nD. One simply feels that it is one of the things that must be\r\nso. <br>\r\nE. No one can say what was the mutual magnetism that\r\nbrought the deathless partnership of these wholesome and\r\npalatable foodstuffs about.       \r\n', 'ACBED ', 'CEDBA	', 'ACEBD  ', 'CEABD', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3008, 260, 2, ' <b>Direction for the questions:</b><br> Find the correct order<br><br>\r\nA. Events intervened, and in the late 1930s and 1940s,\r\nGermany suffered from â€œover-brandingâ€.  <br>      \r\nB. The British used to be fascinated by the home of\r\nRomanticism.        <br>\r\nC. But reunification and the federal governmentâ€™s move to\r\nBerlin have prompted Germany to think again about its\r\nimage.       <br> \r\nD. The first foreign package holiday was a tour of Germany\r\norganized by Thomas Cook in 1855.     <br>   \r\nE. Since then, Germany has been understandably nervous\r\nabout promoting itself abroad.        \r\n', 'ACEBD', 'DECAB	', 'BDAEC', 'DBAEC', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3009, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for the questions:</b><br> Choose the correct\r\nphrase to find out the closest meaning of idiom & phrase. <br><br>\r\nHe has rejoined office after a week and looks run down.\r\n', 'Cheerful and bubbly', 'Weak and tried', 'Active and energetic	', 'Busy and preoccupied', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3010, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for the questions:</b><br> Choose the correct\r\nphrase to find out the closest meaning of idiom & phrase. <br><br>\r\nCharlie doesnâ€™t work like getting too tired so he gets his\r\nassistant to do all the donkey work.\r\n', 'academic work	', 'art work', 'home work	', 'hard work', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3011, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for the questions:</b><br> Choose the correct\r\nphrase to find out the closest meaning of idiom & phrase. <br><br>\r\nWhy do you wish to treads on the toes?\r\n', 'To give offence to them', 'To follow them grudgingly', 'To treat them indifferently', 'To be kicked by them', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3012, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for the questions:</b><br> Choose the correct\r\nphrase to find out the closest meaning of idiom & phrase. <br><br>\r\nBeing the head of a large family he finds it difficult to\r\nkeep his head above water.\r\n', 'To take rest', 'To work properly', 'To avoid quarrel', 'To keep out of debt', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3013, 260, 2, '<b>Directions for the questions:</b><br> Choose the correct\r\nphrase to find out the closest meaning of idiom & phrase. <br><br>\r\nI would advise you to wait and see which way the cat\r\njumps.\r\n', 'what develops 	', 'what goes', 'what comes	', 'what falls', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3014, 261, 2, 'There are 6561 balls out of them 1 is heavy. Find the minimum number of times the balls have to be weighed for finding out the heavy ball. ', '7', '6', '9', '8', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3015, 261, 2, 'If I walk with 30 mph, I reach an hour before and if I walk with 20 mph, I reach an hour late. Find the distance between two points and the exact time of reaching destination is 11 a.m. then find the speed with which it walks. ', '22 mph', '23mph ', '24mph ', '20mph', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3016, 261, 2, 'Ramesh starting from a fixed point goes 15 m towards North and then after turning to his right he goes 15 m. Then he goes 10, 15 and 15 metres after turning to his left each time. How far is he from his starting point? ', '50 m', '10 m    ', '20 m ', '15 m ', 'Indeterminate', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3017, 261, 2, 'John bought a machine to make fresh juice. He has five different fruits: strawberries, oranges, apples, pineapples, and lemons. If he only uses two fruits, how many different \r\njuice drinks can John make?\r\n', '10', '12', '14', '16', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3018, 261, 2, 'If BANANA + GUAVA = ORANGE then what is O+R+A+N+G+E=?', '24', '31', '48', '61', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3019, 261, 2, 'Two goods train each 500 m long, are running in opposite directions on parallel tracks. Their speeds are 45 km/hr and 30 km/hr respectively. Find the time taken by the slower train to pass the driver of the faster one.', '12 Sec', '24 Sec', '48 Sec', '60Sec ', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3020, 261, 2, 'In a class of 50 students, 18 take Chorus, 26 take Band, and 2 take both Chorus and Band.  How many students in the class are not enrolled in either Chorus or Band?', '20', '8', '10', '12', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3021, 261, 2, 'Today is Friday. A lady says â€œyou have to meet me two days after the day before the day after tomorrowâ€. When do you have to meet the lady? ', 'Monday  ', 'Tuesday   ', 'Saturday', 'Sunday ', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3022, 261, 2, 'In a class there are less than 500 students. When it is divided by 3 it gives a whole number. Similarly, when it is divided by 4, 5 or 7, it gives a whole number. What is the minimum number of students in the class?', '400', '350', '420', '500', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3023, 261, 2, 'A coffee seller has two types of coffee Brand A costing 5 bits per pound and Brand B costing 3 bits per pound. \r\nHe mixes two brands to get a 40 pound mixture. He sold this at 6 bits per pound. The seller gets a profit of 33.5%. How much he has used Brand A in the mixture? \r\n', '15 pounds', '30 pounds   ', '20 pounds   ', '10Pounds', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3024, 262, 2, '<b>Direction for questions :</b><br> In each question below are given three Statements followed by three Conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the given Statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read all the conclusions and then decide which of the given Conclusions logically follows from the given Statements disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n<b>STATEMENTS: </b><br>\r\nA. Some cycles are buses. <br>	B. All cars are buses. <br>\r\nC. Some buses are trains. <br>\r\n<b>CONCLUSIONS: </b><br>\r\nI.  All cars are cycles. <br>	 	II. Some trains are buses. <br>\r\nIII. Some trains are cars. <br>\r\n', 'None follows ', 'Only I and II follow ', 'Only l and III follow ', 'Only II and III follow ', 'None of these ', 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1),
(3025, 262, 2, '<b>Direction for questions :</b><br> In each question below are given three Statements followed by three Conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the given Statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read all the conclusions and then decide which of the given Conclusions logically follows from the given Statements disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n<b>STATEMENTS: </b> <br>\r\nA. All pencils are sticks. <br>	B. Some sticks are notes. <br> \r\nC. All diaries are notes. <br>\r\n<b>CONCLUSIONS: </b> <br>\r\nI. Some notes are diaries. <br>	II. Some sticks are pencils. <br>\r\nIII. Some diaries are sticks. \r\n', 'All follow 	', 'Only I follows.', 'Only I and II follow ', 'Only II follows ', 'None of these ', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3026, 262, 2, '<b>Direction for questions :</b><br> In each question below are given three Statements followed by three Conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the given Statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read all the conclusions and then decide which of the given Conclusions logically follows from the given Statements disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>STATEMENTS: </b> <br>\r\nA. Some buds are leaves. <br>	B. No leaf is fruit. <br>\r\nC. Some fruits are buds. <br>\r\n<b>CONCLUSIONS: </b> <br>\r\nI. Some fruits are leaves. <br>	II. All buds are fruits. <br> \r\nIII. Some leaves are buds. \r\n', 'Only I or II follows ', 'Only III follows ', 'Only II follows ', 'None follows ', 'None of these ', 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3027, 262, 2, '<b>Direction for questions :</b><br> In each question below are given three Statements followed by three Conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the given Statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read all the conclusions and then decide which of the given Conclusions logically follows from the given Statements disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\nb>STATEMENTS: </b><br>\r\nA. Some birds are animals. <br>	B. All animals are rivers. <br>\r\nC. Some rivers are lions. <br>\r\n<b>CONCLUSIONS: </b><br>\r\nI. Some lions are animals <br>	II. Some rivers are birds <br>\r\nIII. No animal is lion \r\n', 'Only II follows ', 'Only either I or III follows ', 'I and II follows ', 'Only either II or III follow ', 'None of these ', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3028, 262, 2, '<b>Direction for questions :</b><br> In each question below are given three Statements followed by three Conclusions numbered I, II and III. You have to take the given Statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read all the conclusions and then decide which of the given Conclusions logically follows from the given Statements disregarding commonly known facts. <br><br>\r\n<b>STATEMENTS: </b> <br>\r\nA. All boxes are pans 	<br>	B. Some boxes are jugs. <br>\r\nC. Some jugs are glasses. <br>\r\n<b>CONCLUSIONS: </b><br>\r\nI. Some glasses are boxes <br>	II. No glass is box <br>\r\nIII. Some jugs are pans <br>	IV. No jug is pan \r\n', 'Only I and II follows ', 'Either I or II and III follows ', 'Only III follows ', 'Either I or II, and either III or IV follow ', 'None of these ', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3029, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Each problem consists of a problem followed by two statements. Decide whether the data in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Select your answer according to whether: <br><br>\r\nIf the average size of 3 accounts is $1 million, is the smallest account less than $500,000? <br>\r\nI. The largest account is $1.3 million. <br>\r\nII. One of the accounts is $0.7 million. \r\n', 'Statement I alone is sufficient, but statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Statement II alone is sufficient, but statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Both statements taken together are sufficient to answer the question, but neither statement alone is sufficient ', 'Each statement alone is sufficient ', 'Statements I and II together are not sufficient, and additional data is needed to answer the question', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3030, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Each problem consists of a problem followed by two statements. Decide whether the data in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Select your answer according to whether: <br><br>\r\nIs the product of x and y greater than 60? <br>\r\nI. The sum of x and y is greater than 60. <br>\r\nII. Each of the variables is greater than 2. \r\n', 'Statement I alone is sufficient, but statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Statement II alone is sufficient, but statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Both statements taken together are sufficient to answer the question, but neither statement alone is sufficient ', 'Each statement alone is sufficient ', 'Statements I and II together are not sufficient, and additional data is needed to answer the question', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3031, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Each problem consists of a problem followed by two statements. Decide whether the data in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Select your answer according to whether: <br><br>\r\nWhat is the value of y? <br>	\r\nI. y â€“ 3 = 2 <br>\r\nII. y2 = 25 \r\n', 'Statement I alone is sufficient, but statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Statement II alone is sufficient, but statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Both statements taken together are sufficient to answer the question, but neither statement alone is sufficient ', 'Each statement alone is sufficient ', 'Statements I and II together are not sufficient, and additional data is needed to answer the question', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3032, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Each problem consists of a problem followed by two statements. Decide whether the data in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Select your answer according to whether: <br><br>\r\nWhat was the percent increase of Company A''s stock between June 1 and June 30, 2000? <br>\r\nI. The stock gained $5 in value during June 2000. <br>\r\nII. The stock rose 12% during the first half of the month. \r\n', 'Statement I alone is sufficient, but statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Statement II alone is sufficient, but statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Both statements taken together are sufficient to answer the question, but neither statement alone is sufficient ', 'Each statement alone is sufficient ', 'Statements I and II together are not sufficient, and additional data is needed to answer the question', 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1);
INSERT INTO `qb_options` (`id`, `cat_id`, `difficulty_level`, `question`, `answer1`, `answer2`, `answer3`, `answer4`, `answer5`, `option1`, `option2`, `option3`, `option4`, `option5`, `status`) VALUES
(3033, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Each problem consists of a problem followed by two statements. Decide whether the data in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Select your answer according to whether: <br><br>\r\nWhich company reported the larger dollar increase in earnings? <br>\r\nI. Company A reported that its earnings increased by 5%. <br>\r\nII. Company B reported that its earnings increased by 7%. \r\n', 'Statement I alone is sufficient, but statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Statement II alone is sufficient, but statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question ', 'Both statements taken together are sufficient to answer the question, but neither statement alone is sufficient ', 'Each statement alone is sufficient ', 'Statements I and II together are not sufficient, and additional data is needed to answer the question', 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1),
(3034, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Read the following information carefully and answer the questions given it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are six persons P, Q, R, S, T and U in a school. Each of the teachers teaches two subjects, one compulsory subject and the other optional subject. S''s optional subject was History while there others have it as compulsory subject. T and U have Physics as one of their subjects. U''s compulsory subject is Mathematics which is an optional subject of both R and T. History and English are P''s subjects but in terms of compulsory and optional subjects, they are just reverse of those of S''s. Chemistry is an optional subject of only one of them. There is only one female teacher in the school and she has English as her compulsory subject. <br><br>\r\nWhat is R''s compulsory subject? \r\n', 'History', 'Physics', 'Chemistry', 'English     ', 'Mathematics', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(3035, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Read the following information carefully and answer the questions given it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are six persons P, Q, R, S, T and U in a school. Each of the teachers teaches two subjects, one compulsory subject and the other optional subject. S''s optional subject was History while there others have it as compulsory subject. T and U have Physics as one of their subjects. U''s compulsory subject is Mathematics which is an optional subject of both R and T. History and English are P''s subjects but in terms of compulsory and optional subjects, they are just reverse of those of S''s. Chemistry is an optional subject of only one of them. There is only one female teacher in the school and she has English as her compulsory subject. <br><br>\r\nWho is a female member in the group? \r\n', 'P', 'Q', 'R', 'S', 'T', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3036, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Read the following information carefully and answer the questions given it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are six persons P, Q, R, S, T and U in a school. Each of the teachers teaches two subjects, one compulsory subject and the other optional subject. S''s optional subject was History while there others have it as compulsory subject. T and U have Physics as one of their subjects. U''s compulsory subject is Mathematics which is an optional subject of both R and T. History and English are P''s subjects but in terms of compulsory and optional subjects, they are just reverse of those of S''s. Chemistry is an optional subject of only one of them. There is only one female teacher in the school and she has English as her compulsory subject. <br><br>\r\nWhich of the following has some compulsory and optional subjects as those of U''s? \r\n', 'S', 'Q', 'P', 'R', 'None', 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1),
(3037, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Read the following information carefully and answer the questions given it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are six persons P, Q, R, S, T and U in a school. Each of the teachers teaches two subjects, one compulsory subject and the other optional subject. S''s optional subject was History while there others have it as compulsory subject. T and U have Physics as one of their subjects. U''s compulsory subject is Mathematics which is an optional subject of both R and T. History and English are P''s subjects but in terms of compulsory and optional subjects, they are just reverse of those of S''s. Chemistry is an optional subject of only one of them. There is only one female teacher in the school and she has English as her compulsory subject. <br><br>\r\nDisregarding which is the compulsory and which is the optional subject, who has the same two subject combination as F? \r\n', 'P', 'Q', 'R', 'S', 'None ', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
(3038, 262, 2, '<b>Directions for questions:</b><br> Read the following information carefully and answer the questions given it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are six persons P, Q, R, S, T and U in a school. Each of the teachers teaches two subjects, one compulsory subject and the other optional subject. S''s optional subject was History while there others have it as compulsory subject. T and U have Physics as one of their subjects. U''s compulsory subject is Mathematics which is an optional subject of both R and T. History and English are P''s subjects but in terms of compulsory and optional subjects, they are just reverse of those of S''s. Chemistry is an optional subject of only one of them. There is only one female teacher in the school and she has English as her compulsory subject. <br><br>\r\nWhich of the following groups has History as the compulsory subject? \r\n', 'P, R, S', 'Q, R, S   ', 'R, S   ', 'P, Q, R  ', 'P, S', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(4131, 302, 2, '<b>Directions to Solve</b><br>\r\nWhich of phrases given below each sentence should replace the phrase printed in bold type to make the grammatically correct? If the sentence is correct as it is, mark ''E'' as the answer.\r\n\r\n<br><b>You need not come unless you want to.</b>', 'You don''t need to come unless you want to', 'You come only when you want to', 'You come unless you don''t want to', 'You needn''t come until you don''t want to', 'No correction required', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
(4132, 302, 2, '<b>Directions to Solve</b><br>\r\nWhich of phrases given below each sentence should replace the phrase printed in bold type to make the grammatically correct? If the sentence is correct as it is, mark ''E'' as the answer.<br>\r\n\r\nThere are not many men who are so famous that they are frequently referred to by their <b>short names</b> only', 'initials', 'signatures', 'pictures', 'middle names', 'No correction required', 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
