she says it. The moralist in Lessing, struggling with the very skilled writer, at times has made her writing prolix, clogged, slow-though in her latest novels she has successfully introduced a leavening of fantasy. The fact is that there are writers who in an economical page or two can make us feel our dilemmas more piercingly than she does in a leisurely fictional experience. Missing from her work is that sense of time and space gathered up for a moment between the hands, that sudden shift from understanding to seeing directly, that we expect at rare moments from our storytellers. <br><br>\r\nThe passage suggests that the author admires writers\r\n', 'whose fiction is less militant than Lessingâ€™s', 'who express greater optimism and hope than Lessing does.', 'whose insights are succinctly and trenchantly developed.', 'whose writings have been characterized by stylistic innovation.', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
