(3662, 285, 2, '<B>Directions for question:</B><BR> \r\nRead the information below. <BR>\r\nAkash, Basu, and Chandra are residents of Narad Nagar.  The residents have a strange habit, they lie about themselves but not about their opinion of other residents. All residents are aware of each otherâ€™s habit. Four automobile companies test market their products in Narad Nagar.  <BR>Twelve cars viz. Zen, Santro, Matiz, and Indica in colours blue, red, and green each are randomly distributed among them and they all get equal number of cars. None of them know what car the other two gets. <BR><BR>\r\nAkash says that not more than 3 of his cars are of the same colour.  On hearing this, Basu concludes that Chandra has a blue Matiz. This implies<BR>\r\nI.   Basu has two Matiz cars. <BR>\r\nII.  Basu has at least one blue car. <BR>\r\nIII. Basu has one Matiz. <BR>\r\nIV. Akash has only green coloured cars.\r\n', 'I and II', 'II and IV', 'II and III       ', 'Only II', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
(3663, 285, 2, '<B>Directions for question:</B><BR> \r\nRead the information below. <BR>\r\nAkash, Basu, and Chandra are residents of Narad Nagar.  The residents have a strange habit, they lie about themselves but not about their opinion of other residents. All residents are aware of each otherâ€™s habit. Four automobile companies test market their products in Narad Nagar.  <BR>Twelve cars viz. Zen, Santro, Matiz, and Indica in colours blue, red, and green each are randomly distributed among them and they all get equal number of cars. None of them know what car the other two gets. <BR><BR>\r\nBasu says that Chandra has no green cars.  Hearing this Akash concludes that Chandra must have four blue cars.  It can be inferred that<BR>\r\nI. Basu has all green cars. <BR>\r\nII. Akash has three blue and one red car. <BR>\r\nIII. Chandra has the blue Santro. <BR>\r\nIV. Akash has four red cars.\r\n', 'Only I', 'Only III', 'I, III, IV         ', 'Only II', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1),
